From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: raw mouse movement for pointer lock on X11 (KccX11RawMouse)

Stock: aura::WindowTreeHost::RequestUnadjustedMovement()
(ui/aura/window_tree_host.cc) is "NOTIMPLEMENTED(); return nullptr;" and
only DesktopWindowTreeHostWin overrides it.  On Linux
RenderWidgetHostViewEventHandler::LockPointer() therefore answers
requestPointerLock({unadjustedMovement: true}) with kUnsupportedOptions,
the game falls back to a plain lock, and movementX/Y are differences of
pointer positions: accelerated by the X server / libinput, and produced by
warping the hidden pointer back to the middle of the window.

Change: with the feature enabled on X11 the request succeeds and, while
the returned scoper lives, the locked window gets mouse moves flagged
ui::EF_UNADJUSTED_MOUSE whose movement() is the unaccelerated device delta
from XInput2 XI_RawMotion.  ui::MakeWebMouseEvent() already turns that
flag into WebMouseEvent::is_raw_movement_event + movement_x/y on every
platform, so nothing in content/ or blink changes.

Layers:
  - DesktopWindowTreeHostLinux::RequestUnadjustedMovement() (new
    override; ui/views/widget/desktop_aura).  Checks the feature, asks the
    window's X11Extension, returns a scoper that turns raw motion off
    again through a WeakPtr to the host.  Otherwise it calls the base
    class, i.e. stock.  Wayland windows have no X11Extension: stock.
  - ui::X11Extension::SetRawMouseMotionEnabled(bool) (new virtual with a
    default "return false"; X11Window is the only implementer in tree).
    X11Extension was chosen over PlatformWindow so that no other platform
    sees a new method.
  - X11Window (ui/ozone/platform/x11) does the work.
  - DeviceDataManagerX11::GetRawMotionDelta() (ui/events/devices/x11)
    decodes the event next to the existing Fp3232/valuator helpers.

Details:
  - Selection.  XI_RawMotion only goes to clients that select it on the
    root window.  X11Window selects {root, DeviceId::AllMaster, RawMotion}
    when the first window enables raw motion and writes an empty mask when
    the last one disables it (file-static counter; also from
    CloseXWindow()).  The only other XI2 selection this client has on the
    root is DeviceDataManagerX11's Hierarchy|DeviceChanged for
    DeviceId::All, which is a separate mask in the server.
  - One event per motion.  The server processes a raw event for the slave
    and again for the master it is attached to; selecting for AllMaster
    delivers only the master copy, and GetRawMotionDelta() additionally
    requires deviceid to be one of master_pointers().
  - Decoding.  Only axisvalues_raw (unaccelerated), only valuators 0 and 1
    as present in valuator_mask; scroll valuators are ignored.  Events
    whose source device (sourceid) has X or Y in ValuatorMode::Absolute
    are ignored: tablets, touchscreens, VM pointers, and XWayland's
    absolute pointer.  The set of such devices is kept by
    DeviceDataManagerX11::UpdateDeviceList().
  - Delivery.  X11Window::OnEvent() (x11::EventObserver, UI thread) hands
    the delta to OnRawMouseMotion(), which builds the same thing
    HWNDMessageHandler::OnInputEvent() builds on Windows: a kMouseMoved
    ui::MouseEvent, time EventTimeForNow(), DispatcherApi::set_movement(),
    current modifier/button flags, sent through
    DispatchEventFromNativeUiEvent() ->
    PlatformWindowDelegate::DispatchEvent() like every other event.  Raw
    motion has no position; the centre of the window is reported.
    Modifier/button flags are copied from the latest ordinary mouse event
    because raw events carry none.
  - Sub-pixel.  WebMouseEvent::movement_x/y are ints.  The fractional part
    of the raw delta is kept in a double per axis and added to the next
    event, so slow movement is not truncated away.
  - No double counting.  NOTE: at this baseline
    RenderWidgetHostViewEventHandler does not drop ordinary moves while
    unadjusted movement is on; on Windows HWNDMessageHandler does it
    ("Suppress kMouseMoved and kMouseDragged ... when using WM_INPUT").
    X11Window does the same in DispatchUiEvent(): once the first usable
    raw motion has arrived and while the window holds the capture,
    kMouseMoved/kMouseDragged built from ordinary X motion are not
    dispatched.  Buttons, wheel and keys are untouched.
  - Fallback.  Until a usable raw motion arrives, ordinary moves are
    dispatched as today.  A pointer that never produces one (absolute
    device only, or XWayland on a compositor without the relative pointer
    protocol) therefore behaves exactly like a plain lock, although the
    page was told unadjustedMovement is active.
  - Re-centring.  The content layer re-centres the pointer from the
    positions of ordinary moves, which it no longer sees.  X11Window does
    it instead: when a dropped ordinary move is outside the central half
    of the window it warps the pointer to the centre.  Warps generate an
    ordinary motion event (dropped) and no raw motion (the server queues
    them with POINTER_NORAW).

Focus: raw motion is delivered by the server regardless of focus and of
grabs.  It is forwarded only while the window holds the capture that
WindowTreeHostPlatform::LockMouse() takes.  When the widget loses focus
RenderWidgetHostImpl unlocks the pointer, the scoper dies and the root
selection is removed.

XWayland: raw motion comes from zwp_relative_pointer_v1 (unaccelerated
values) while the pointer is over the window; XWayland keeps the pointer
locked only while the client keeps warping a hidden pointer, which the
re-centring above does, as the stock code does.  If the compositor has no
relative pointer protocol no usable raw motion arrives and the stock
behaviour stays.

What users will see: movementX/Y in device counts, as on Windows, instead
of accelerated pixels, so in-game sensitivity has to be set again; no
change with the feature off.

Gating: base::Feature KccX11RawMouse, disabled by default, file local in
desktop_window_tree_host_linux.cc.  With it off the override returns the
base class result (nullptr after NOTIMPLEMENTED(), as stock) and no
X11Window code path changes: raw_mouse_motion_enabled_ is never set.  The
only unconditional addition is the absolute-device set filled in
UpdateDeviceList(), which nothing reads.

A/B: --ozone-platform=x11 --enable-features=KccX11RawMouse.  In devtools,
  await document.body.requestPointerLock({unadjustedMovement: true})
resolves with the feature and rejects (NotSupportedError) without.  Then
compare the sum of movementX over a fixed physical distance at different
hand speeds (must not depend on speed with the feature), and at different
"xinput set-prop ... libinput Accel Speed" values (must not change).
"xinput test-xi2 --root" shows the RawMotion values to compare against.
"xdotool mousemove_relative" injects relative raw motion for scripted
tests.  Check under XWayland as well, and that alt-tab and Escape leave
the mouse usable.

Known limits:
  - Absolute motion injected through XTEST ("xdotool mousemove", x11vnc,
    Barrier/Synergy in absolute mode) arrives as raw motion from a device
    with relative axes; its coordinates are taken as a delta.
  - Once a relative device has produced raw motion, an absolute device
    used during the same lock does not move anything.
  - Downgrading a held lock to adjusted movement (requestPointerLock()
    again without the option) can give one jump of up to a quarter of the
    window, because the pointer is somewhere in the central half.
  - If the web contents do not cover the middle of the X window (docked
    devtools), the content layer's own re-centring also runs: extra
    warps, movement is still counted once.
  - One raw sample can be discarded by the renderer right after the
    content layer itself warps the pointer (lock taken with the pointer
    near the window edge while the mouse is moving), because the next
    move at the centre is taken for the warp.  Do not "fix" this by
    extending the IS_WIN-only early return for unadjusted movement in
    RenderWidgetHostViewEventHandler::ShouldMoveToCenter() to Linux: the
    fallback path above depends on the content layer re-centring.
  - Every raw event is dispatched on its own, as on Windows.  8 kHz mice
    will cost browser UI thread time; raw events are not coalesced here.
  - Found in a desk-check on 2026-11-21, fixed on the Linux build on
    2026-11-24: SetRawMouseMotionEnabled(true) reset raw_mouse_event_flags_
    to 0, and only ordinary mouse events refresh it.  The server queues a
    motion's raw event before its core motion event, so when the lock was
    taken from a mousedown and the mouse moved with the button still held,
    the first move made from raw motion carried no button or modifier
    flags: a kMouseMoved instead of kMouseDragged, and the page saw
    buttons == 0 (and lost a held Shift/Ctrl) on that one mousemove.  The
    field is now seeded on enable with XGetMaskAsEventFlags() &
    kRawMouseMotionStateFlags (ui/base/x/x11_drag_drop_client.h, already
    used in this file; one synchronous QueryPointer; Shift, Ctrl, Alt,
    Super and the left, middle and right buttons.  Caps Lock and Num Lock
    are not in that mask and stay off until the next ordinary mouse event,
    as before).

Not verified: nothing was compiled or run.  Type and field names were
taken from ui/gfx/x/generated_protos/xinput.h, which is checked in and is
what the build compiles unless regenerate_x11_protos is set.  X server
behaviour quoted above (master/slave copies, POINTER_NORAW for warps, raw
delivery under grabs for XI >= 2.1 clients, XWayland) was read from the
xorg-server sources, not tested.

Platforms: Linux/X11 (and XWayland).  The views and platform_window files
also build for Wayland, where nothing changes.

Focus gate (added in review): XI_RawMotion is selected on the root window, so
the server delivers it whatever application has the focus. OnRawMouseMotion()
forwards it only while this window both holds the capture and IsActive() (has
the X input focus), and PreHandleMouseEventForRawMotion() suppresses ordinary
moves under the same condition. Without the focus check, movement made in
another application could reach the page in the window between focus loss and
pointer lock being torn down. When the gate is closed the sub-pixel remainder
is cleared and ordinary mouse moves flow as in a plain lock.

Build and check status (2026-11-21)
  - Not compiled: Linux-only files, not part of a Windows build.
    Desk-checked against this tree (identifiers, xinput protos, overrides,
    implementers, gn deps): nothing found that would fail; one minor logic
    bug, listed under "Known limits" above.
  - Not checked: everything; it needs a Linux X11 build.
The "not compiled" / "not verified" notes above were written before the
build; they stay for the caveats they list. Details and raw logs:
patches/electron-45/RESULTS.md and results/.

